laurel 3051: LAUREL CPU MARGIN: the top OSM row of each cluster is held back (silver 1612 MHz, gold 1804 MHz on the Mi A3); laurelcpumargin=0. Both kernels. docs/laurel_sprout/watchdog.md section 16.

[V] 26 stuck-core catches since 2026-11-03, the day 2972 pinned both clusters at their top rows (1804 and 2016 MHz) whenever a core is
full; 11-06: 16 in 65 min, each in the boot storm (both clusters at 100 %); 11-07: 4 in 7 minutes at 76.7-79.3 C with both clusters at
100 %, then a total hang; the quiet 34 minutes of that boot ran mostly at low load. Always the same physical core of a cluster: the mark
of a core with the smallest margin at an operating point, not of a thread (26 processes, 26 user and kernel pcs). The OSM sets the
voltage of a row by its own table, so the top rows are legal, but stock Android never holds both clusters there for minutes (its thermal
zones throttle at the skin sensors first); one row down is where stock spends its hot time. [I] cause; the catch rate with and without
this patch on the same use decides it. Self-check: 3 rows in both tables. The thermal caps (2303) now rise to the row in use, not to the
table's last row; the on-line of LAUREL CPUFREQ names the rows in use. Rule lgov_top_row() in laurel_gov_core.h (gov-test.sh).

